#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-gh: one shared GitHub CI watcher for every agent session on this machine.

    glaeda-gh serve                                     the daemon (LaunchAgent or systemd user unit)
    glaeda-gh status pr OWNER/REPO#N [--json]           PR state, checks, mergeability, review
    glaeda-gh status pr OWNER/REPO#A OWNER/REPO#B C ...  several PRs in one gather (a bare N, or a quoted '#N',
                                                        reuses the repository before it); --json gives a list
    glaeda-gh status pr ... --field head,checks.failed  just those values, tab-separated, one line per PR
    glaeda-gh status run OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID [--jobs] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait pr OWNER/REPO#N [--until green|done|required|merged] [--timeout S] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait run OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID [--jobs] [--rescue-grace S] [--timeout S] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait comment OWNER/REPO#N [--author LOGIN] [--match REGEX] [--since ID|URL|TIME|10m]
    glaeda-gh status comment OWNER/REPO#N               the latest comments on an issue or PR
    glaeda-gh wait file PATH [--exists|--gone] [--contains REGEX] [--timeout S]   a file, instead of a sleep loop
    glaeda-gh wait pid PID [--timeout S]                a process to exit, instead of a `kill -0` loop
    glaeda-gh checks OWNER/REPO SHA... --consumer NAME [--max-age S] [--wait S] [--json]
                                                        every check of those commits, read once for all sessions
    glaeda-gh post comment OWNER/REPO#N --body-file F|- [--wait S]    queue a comment; the daemon posts it, paced
    glaeda-gh post pr OWNER/REPO --head B --base B --title T --body-file F [--reviewer LOGIN] [--wait S]
    glaeda-gh post review OWNER/REPO#N [--event comment|approve|request-changes] --body-file F [--wait S]
    glaeda-gh post status [ID] [--wait S] [--json]      one write's URL or error, or the queue and recent writes
    glaeda-gh budget [--json]                           REST and GraphQL quota as last seen
    glaeda-gh read < REQUEST                            cached entries for an upstream client (upstream.json)
    glaeda-gh restart [--force]                         restart a down or hung daemon, once for every session
    glaeda-gh gh ARG...                                 one `gh` call, brokered: served from the cache when it
                                                        can be, refused when it reads local source through the
                                                        API, otherwise passed to the real gh unchanged; an
                                                        identical read in the next 60 s gets the same answer
                                                        (GLAEDA_GH_CACHE_TTL, 0 turns it off)
    glaeda-gh gh --install-shim                         put that in front of `gh` on PATH (--print-shim to look)
    glaeda-gh gh --probe                                whether this glaeda-gh has the broker, for a wrapper
                                                        deciding whether to hand its calls over
    glaeda-gh audit [--log PATH] [--last N] [--json]    this host's gh traffic, and what a cache would answer
    glaeda-gh runners OWNER[/REPO] [--label L] [--json] busy and idle self-hosted runners, from one shared read
                                                        (instead of a jobs read per run: see GH_SWEEP_MAX)

Many sessions share one GitHub account and so one REST quota (5000 requests an hour). Each
session polling its own PRs and runs used it up while the separate GraphQL quota sat idle. Here
the daemon is the only process that talks to GitHub, and clients only read its cache:

- Clients register interest by writing a small file in the watch directory; its mtime is the
  last-interest time. An entry nobody has read for 15 minutes is dropped, and a merged, closed or
  completed one 2 minutes after its last reader. A PR or run watch whose only interest was one look
  (`status`, a sweep over many PRs) or a wait that has ended is dropped as soon as the daemon has read it
  since: the watch set is what live waits and repeat readers need, not every PR anyone looked at.
- Every watched PR is read in batched GraphQL queries each cycle (60 s), including the rate limit.
  GitHub answers 504 to a query that runs past ~10 s, so a batch starts at 10 PRs, halves on a 504
  or timeout and retries the halves at once, and grows back by one after 5 clean queries. A PR
  that times out alone is read alone until it answers. `budget` shows the batch size and the
  oldest data of an open watch.
  A check that failed gets its failed steps from one more small query, once: a completed check
  does not change.
- Runs use REST with If-None-Match, so an unchanged run answers 304, which GitHub does not count
  against the quota. The job list is read when a client passed --jobs, and once per attempt when
  a run fails, for the failed steps.
- REST and GraphQL are one pool. Each tick, PR reads and run reads each go to whichever bucket is
  richer for the time it has left: what remains above its reserve divided by the seconds until
  its own reset (600 left with 2 minutes to go is rich; 600 left with 50 is poor). A kind moves
  only when the other bucket is SWITCH_MARGIN richer, so it does not flap, and both buckets drain
  about evenly. Runs over GraphQL are one aliased `resource(url:)` query per batch of runs (job lists
  stay on REST: only REST names the runner, which tells a refusal from a failure). PRs over REST
  read the pull, its head's check runs, statuses and workflow runs, the workflow names, and the
  base branch's required checks, every one with If-None-Match; REST has no review decision, so the last one
  GraphQL read is kept, as are the required checks on a private repository whose plan answers 403 for
  rulesets and branch protection. A bucket below its floor moves its kinds to the other bucket when
  that one is not, whatever the margin. Comment watches and heads (`checks`) stay on GraphQL, and move
  to REST only while GraphQL is below its floor or throttled and REST is not. Within a tick, reads a
  GraphQL query could not make because the quota ran out go over REST, and reads REST could not make
  (its quota, or the share) go over GraphQL. A secondary-limit 403 holds both buckets instead: the
  limit is one for both, so moving the reads would be the burst it refuses. The hold is Retry-After or
  60 s doubling to 15 min, whichever is longer, and no read is sent during it.
- Comment watches (an issue's or PR's latest 50 comments) are one batched GraphQL query every 15 s
  (over REST: the issue and the page or two holding them), and last only while someone waits on them.
- A merged or closed PR's failed steps are not read (its cached ones are kept), and a job whose steps read GitHub
  refused for good (404, 410, 422) is not asked again for STEPS_RETRY (15 minutes); a 5xx is asked again next read.
- Reads follow interest. A PR nobody has waited on or read for IDLE_BACKOFF_AFTER (5 minutes) is polled about
  as often as it was last read, up to every 10 minutes, and its check events no longer re-read it early; a read
  (a `status`, a wait, the broker answering from the cache) puts it back on every cycle. A comment wait on a
  repository the controller's webhooks cover (its feed answered within a minute and delivered an event for
  that repository within the hour) gets new comments from the feed and polls GitHub every 2 minutes as a
  safety net; a running head there is re-read on its own check events and otherwise every 3 minutes, and a head
  due within 30 s rides along in the query of one due now. daemon.json's graphqlBy says what the last hour's
  queries read (pr, run, comment, head, steps, head-pages) and the points GitHub charged for them.
- The X-RateLimit headers and the GraphQL rateLimit field drive a budget guard: below the floor
  (REST 1500 left, GraphQL 500; the fleet config raises GraphQL to 1500) the daemon polls four times less
  often and skips job lists; below the reserve (REST 300, GraphQL 100; the fleet config: 1000) it stops that
  API until the quota resets. The quota is shared, so this also protects every other tool on the account:
  the fleet's GraphQL 1000 is kept for lands (merges and
  the gate reads lane VMs land through). The daemon's own GraphQL spend is also held to graphqlPerMinute
  (40) points in any 60 s; past it the rest of a tick's reads wait, first next tick, so a refresh of every
  entry after a reset, a re-exec or a stop is spread over minutes instead of sent at once. GitHub answers one token's REST calls from
  two "core" counters with their own hourly windows (run and workflow reads report one; /rate_limit,
  pulls and job lists mostly the other), so budget.json keeps each live counter in restCounters and "rest"
  is the poorest of them, never just the last answer. The counter the Actions endpoints answer from
  (ACTIONS_COUNTER_PATH) is tagged "counters": ["actions"], so the gh guard holds each call to its own.
- The daemon's own REST polling is capped at restShare (0.4) of the hourly REST limit: the read
  requests it sent over the last hour that GitHub counted (a 304 is free and is not counted, nor is
  the outbox -- writes sessions asked for and their dedupe lookups -- which the share never holds),
  kept per minute in rest-spend.json across restarts.
  A read starts only when its worst case fits. At the cap, PR and run reads move to GraphQL, or wait
  while GraphQL is stopped too, so the shared REST pool is left to `gh`, landers and nightly re-runs.
  The one read the cap never holds back is a failed attempt's job list (once per attempt, and not below REST's
  reserve): a wait cannot tell a runner's refusal from a failure without it, so it is bounded by failures, not
  polling. Otherwise the share is a hard cap: while GraphQL is low the daemon reads over GraphQL at the lean pace, and while GraphQL is
  stopped too it serves what it holds and waits; it never spends the shared pool past its share (restYield, default
  0, is an operator's local override, and the fleet config may not set it). A yield of the cap again (the old default)
  doubled every daemon's REST spend in the hours GraphQL was low. The share
  is per daemon, so the fleet splits it: config/gh-daemon.json gives each host its share (a host it
  does not name gets 0 and reads PRs and runs over GraphQL, all but that job list), and its shares
  sum to at most 0.4. The cycle, the floors and the share are read every tick from that file
  (installed to ~/.local/share/glaeda/gh-daemon.json; its default, then hosts[update.json's host])
  with ~/.config/glaeda/gh-daemon.json laid over it, {"cycle": 60, "commentCycle": 15, "restShare": 0.4,
  "floors": {"rest": {"floor": 1500, "reserve": 300}, "graphql": {"floor": 500, "reserve": 100}}};
  a missing key or a value out of range keeps its default.
- Spend by path. Every request the daemon sends is logged per minute in spend.json (an hour kept, across
  restarts): REST by route template (GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{id}, the query's parameter names
  only), GraphQL points by query name (pr, run, last-real-attempt, ...), each with its caller, the watch kind and
  repository (or outbox, a session's queued write), and its waiter: each watch file names its readers
  (session:NAME, tool:NAME, or HOST/READER for a lane VM's read through an upstream or a wait over reach; a helper
  inside a session adds :agent:ID, from GLAEDA_GH_AGENT, CMUX_AGENT_NAME or its checkout), and a read is charged to
  the one that read it last. A route's waiters are the PRs' readers, one read per PR or commit whatever the number
  of waits on it: a REST read of a commit, a pull or a run's job list serves every watch on it (repo_parts). daemon.json's spendBy holds the hour's top routes and callers,
  which `budget` prints and the fleet's quota readers (gh_quota.py spenders, ci-dash) can read with no API call.
- Alarms, before the pool is empty rather than after. The pool alarm (policy "alarms": {"pool": true}, on one
  host: the pool is the account's) fires when the poorest REST counter is under alarms.restLeft (1000), its
  drain over the last minutes runs it dry before its reset, or that drain is past alarms.paceShare (0.5) of the
  hourly limit (42 a minute of 5000); it names the host's top REST callers (gh-quota usage) as well as this
  daemon's routes and their waiters. The share alarm (every host) when this daemon's own
  REST pace over the last 10 minutes is past its cap by half. Each is a notice line in
  ~/.local/state/notices/glaeda-gh.jsonl and a `tell-coordinator --from glaeda-gh` (WARN, or FIRE under the
  reserve or within 10 minutes of dry), naming the top routes; at most once an hour unless it gets worse.
- The token comes from `gh auth token` and stays in memory; it is never written or logged.
- A host with no GitHub login of its own (a VM that may hold no token) can read through an upstream
  instead: ~/.config/glaeda/upstream.json, {"command": ["PROGRAM", "ARG", ...]}, names a program that
  carries `read` requests to another host's glaeda-gh (a broker that daemon's host serves, say). The
  daemon then makes no GitHub request at all: every UPSTREAM_EVERY seconds, and UPSTREAM_NEW after a
  key appears with no data yet, it runs that program once with every watched key as one JSON line on
  stdin, {"keys": [{"key": "pr:o/r#1", "jobs": false}, ...]}, and caches what it prints,
  {"entries": {KEY: {"data", "dataAt", "error", "jobs"?} or null}}, keeping the upstream's dataAt. That
  host's daemon is the one poller, so any number of such hosts cost the quota nothing more than its own
  watches. `post` and `checks` need a login, so their requests fail with that reason; the controller is
  not read. `glaeda-gh read` is the other end: one request on stdin, the cached entries on stdout.
- The cmux build controller on the tailnet (cmuxterm-hq#675) receives GitHub's workflow_run,
  check_suite and issue_comment webhooks. Each tick the daemon reads its change feed once: free,
  so a finished run shows up within about TICK seconds and costs no quota, a check event on a
  watched PR's head commit or branch re-reads that PR at once (at most every NUDGE_MIN seconds),
  and a new comment reaches comment waits. While the controller tracks a run, REST reads it only
  as a safety net every 15 minutes; when the controller is unreachable or has never seen the run,
  the REST path above applies unchanged. Public repositories need no token; private ones need the
  controller token. Controller: $CMUX_CI_CONTROLLER, else the cmux-ci client config, else the
  fleet default; token: $CMUX_CI_TOKEN_FILE, else ~/.config/cmux/build-fleet/controller.token.

- `checks` is the shared check read. A client names itself (--consumer autoland, or a session's name)
  and lists commits; the daemon reads each commit once, in batched GraphQL queries, for every consumer
  that asked, and keeps the answer: a commit whose checks all finished for HEAD_SETTLED_TTL (10 min),
  one with checks running for HEAD_PENDING_TTL (90 s), and only while someone asked in the last 10
  minutes. Each consumer has an hourly budget of head reads (the read is charged to the first consumer
  that may pay, reserved ones first, and shared by the rest). A reserved consumer (autoland, 1500 an
  hour) is served even when the GraphQL quota is lean, whatever the others used; the others share a
  pool (600 an hour, 240 each) and see the cache alone once it is spent or the quota is lean. Tune
  with ~/.config/glaeda/gh-consumers.json: {"reserved": {"autoland": 1500}, "limit": 240, "pool": 600}.
  A head the daemon may not read is served stale when it has an older answer, else listed as unread
  ("budget spent" or "not read yet"); `checks` exits 5 then. `budget` shows each consumer's spend.
- `post` is the one writer. GitHub's secondary limit on content creation is account-wide, so sessions that each
  post and retry on their own ("was submitted too quickly") keep tripping it for all of them. `post` writes the
  comment, PR or review to outbox/ and returns (fire and forget: the write survives the session; --wait S
  prints the URL once posted); the daemon sends one write per tick, POST_SPACING apart, oldest first. A
  throttled answer (403/422/429 naming the secondary limit, or with Retry-After) holds the whole outbox for
  Retry-After, else POST_BACKOFF doubling to POST_BACKOFF_MAX, and never stops the reads. A transport failure
  or 5xx is retried POST_ATTEMPTS times, and a write whose answer was lost is looked up first (the open PR for
  its head, a comment or review with the same body) so it never lands twice. The result (URL, or why it
  failed) stays in posted/ for `post status ID`.
  A trusted exact-head cmux review receipt also wakes its existing risk grader immediately: one fresh
  PR read, then removal of the current risk tier (the grader restores it). Delivery and `riskRegrade`
  are recorded separately; a failed or uncertain label effect is reported without replaying the receipt.
  Comments are cheaper three ways. Comments for one thread already queued together (within POST_JOIN_WINDOW)
  go out as one, each body whole and in order under a rule, and every caller gets that comment's URL; a body
  that a wait, a bot or a reader keys on (a `<!--` marker, a slash command, an @mention, a review receipt) is
  never joined, nor another writer's, nor any on a thread a comment wait watches. A comment goes through GraphQL's addComment while
  GraphQL is the richer bucket (the outbox's own reading: the daemon's REST share never holds writes). And a
  comment queued with --status-key KEY edits the same writer's earlier KEY comment in place, over GraphQL,
  while that is still the thread's last comment and no comment wait watches the thread. Comment waits see an
  edit as new: a comment counts as said when it was created or last edited.
- `wait file` and `wait pid` poll the local disk or process table once a second in one process. They
  exist so no session writes a sleep loop (the polling hook refuses those).

Clients make no API calls. When the daemon is not running they say so and exit 3 rather than
fall back to polling. `wait` exits 0 on success (or a matching comment), 1 on failure (a failed
check, a failed run, a PR closed without being merged, an issue that does not exist), 2 on timeout,
4 when the PR conflicts with its base, 6 when no check or status ever reported on the PR's head
(--settle seconds after its push, default SETTLE_GRACE: its CI has not started, which is not a pass).
--until green (default) fails as soon as any completed check
failed, required or not, and succeeds once every check finished with success, neutral or skipped;
a pending commit status is unfinished like a running check, and so is one of the repo's
EXPECTED_CONTEXTS (leo-workspace's ws-check) that has not been posted yet;
done waits for every check to finish; required lets only the checks GitHub requires decide (when
any are marked); merged waits for the merge.

A PR that conflicts with its base (mergeable CONFLICTING, merge state DIRTY), for example after the
PR it was stacked on was squash-merged, runs no pull_request workflows until the next push. `wait
pr --until green|done|required` therefore stops with exit 4 as soon as the daemon's cached data says so;
--until merged keeps waiting. UNKNOWN mergeability (GitHub computes it lazily) counts as pending.

A job a glaeda runner refused (its "Set up runner" step, the runner's job-started hook, failed
within six minutes; a GUI job may wait up to four for the gui token first) is usually re-run
elsewhere by the repository's own rescue. `wait` holds such a failure as pending for
--rescue-grace seconds (default 360) and follows the next attempt; a failure with any other
cause decides at once.

State lives in ~/.cache/glaeda/gh (GLAEDA_GH_DIR overrides): watch/, cache/, budget.json,
daemon.json (a heartbeat and the last hour's request counts), spend.json, alarms.json and daemon.lock, which the daemon
holds for its lifetime and names itself in. Every GitHub request has a hard deadline (HTTP_DEADLINE),
every subprocess one too, and a tick starts no request after TICK_BUDGET (what it did not reach goes
first next tick). A watchdog thread exits the daemon with EXIT_WATCHDOG, printing every thread's
stack to stderr, when its loop has not come round for WATCHDOG_AFTER, and launchd or systemd starts
a new one. A daemon waiting for the lock stops a holder silent for STALE_HEARTBEAT (a live glaeda-gh
serve, checked first) and takes over. `serve` refuses to start from a shell while the LaunchAgent or
systemd unit is loaded, unless given --foreground-ok: sessions never run their own. A cycle that raises still writes the heartbeat, with the error's type and
code site under "failing"; once cycles have failed for FAILING_AFTER seconds, clients exit 3 and
name the error instead of waiting on a daemon that refreshes nothing. The daemon re-executes itself when this file changes, so an update takes
effect without a restart.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import base64
import contextlib
import datetime as dt
import faulthandler
import fcntl
import glob
import hashlib
from http.client import HTTPException  # the http() helper below shadows the `http` package
import json
import math
import os
import plistlib
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
import traceback
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Callable

BASE = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_GH_DIR") or Path.home() / ".cache/glaeda/gh")
API = "https://api.github.com"
CYCLE = 60  # seconds between polls of a live PR or run
COMMENT_CYCLE = 15  # comment watches: someone is waiting for a reply, and one batched query costs ~1 point
TICK = 2  # how often the daemon looks for new interest; a new entry is filled within one tick
IDLE_EXPIRY = 15 * 60  # drop an entry nobody has read for this long
TERMINAL_GRACE = 120  # drop a merged, closed or completed entry (or a comment watch) this long after its last reader
TERMINAL_REFRESH = 600  # a terminal entry still watched is re-read this rarely (a rerun reopens a run)
CACHE_KEEP = 3600  # unwatched cache files are removed after this long
# A PR or run watch lives only while something live needs it (Daemon.once_answered): one ask that nobody renewed
# this long after the first, or a wait's own last touch after the wait ended, is dropped once the daemon has read it.
ONCE_SPAN = CYCLE
ONCE_SLACK = 2.0  # a wait's touch writes waitedAt and the file's mtime a moment apart
LEAN_FACTOR = 4  # below the floor, poll this many times less often
FLOORS = {"rest": (1500, 300), "graphql": (500, 100)}  # (floor, reserve) per quota: the module defaults
# The fleet's config/gh-daemon.json raises GraphQL's to floor 1500, reserve 1000: the share of the shared login's hour
# kept for lands. Below it the daemon sends no GraphQL read (its PR reads go over REST, or wait for the reset), so the
# merges and gate reads lane VMs land through on the broker host never meet an hour the daemon's polling emptied. On
# 2026-11-04 the account's /graphql counter hit 0 minutes before each :26 reset and the lane-gh broker refused
# cmux#34340's and #34805's lands for it (04:23Z, 05:21Z, 05:22Z).
# The daemon's own GraphQL points in any 60 s, at most. Spread over the minute instead of spent at once: after a
# reset (or a re-exec, or a stop below the reserve) every watched PR is due in the same tick, and a burst of them is
# what the shared login cannot spend for lands. Past it the reads wait for the next tick, first in line (deferred).
GRAPHQL_PER_MINUTE = 40
# The daemon's own counted REST requests in the last hour stay under this share of the hourly limit.
# Measured 2026-10-27: the daemon spent 2549 REST requests of one hour's 5000 (756 more were free
# 304s) on PR reads the balancer had sent to REST, and the account ran out under a nightly re-run.
REST_SHARE = 0.4
# What the share may yield past its cap in an hour while GraphQL is out (share_yields): nothing, unless a host's local
# config sets restYield. The share is a hard cap; running out of GraphQL never spends the shared REST pool. A yield was
# the daemon's biggest REST spend: on 2026-11-10 at 01:35Z cosmic-gray's daemon had 601 counted REST in the hour
# against its cap of 300 (unbounded: 873 in the hour before the pool hit 0), and bounded to the cap again (at least
# 150, so a host with share 0 spent 150 too) every daemon still ran at about twice its share: cosmic-gray 548 to 613
# an hour on a cap of 300, huge-blue 233 on 150 (hq#8948). Past the share, reads go over GraphQL at the lean pace,
# or wait while GraphQL is stopped, served from the cache.
REST_YIELD_DEFAULT = 0
DAEMON_CONFIG = Path.home() / ".config/glaeda/gh-daemon.json"  # cycle, commentCycle, floors, restShare, forceLow
# The fleet's copy of config/gh-daemon.json (glaeda-mini-setup installs it): default, and hosts[NAME]
# laid over it, under DAEMON_CONFIG. NAME is update.json's host, the name the fleet enrolled this host by.
FLEET_POLICY = Path.home() / ".local/share/glaeda/gh-daemon.json"
UPDATE_CONFIG = Path.home() / ".config/glaeda/update.json"
REST_SPEND_FILE = "rest-spend.json"  # the daemon's counted REST requests per minute, kept across restarts
# What the daemon spent by path: per minute, REST requests by route template (GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{id})
# and GraphQL points by query name (pr, run, last-real-attempt, ...), each with the caller that made it (the watch
# kind and repository, or outbox for a session's queued write). rest-spend.json says how much; this says on what.
# On 2026-11-09 the daemon spent 873 counted REST in an hour against its cap of 300 and nothing said which reads.
SPEND_FILE = "spend.json"
SPEND_SAVE_EVERY = 15.0  # seconds between writes of spend.json (rest-spend.json is the crash-safe count)
SPEND_TOP = 10  # rows per table in daemon.json spendBy and `budget`
SPEND_RECENT = 600  # seconds: the window an alarm names the top routes of, and the daemon's own pace is read over
# The alarm: before the shared pool runs dry, not after. Sent with tell-coordinator (the fleet tools' alarm path:
# leo-workspace tools/coord_inbox.py, delivered to the coordinator through acpmux) and written as a notice line
# (~/.local/state/notices/glaeda-gh.jsonl, the schema of cmuxterm-hq tools/fleet-reconcilers/notices.py), at most
# once per ALARM_EVERY for one alarm unless it gets worse. Policy "alarms" ({"pool": bool, "share": bool,
# "restLeft": N, "paceShare": 0..1, 0 for off}) turns each on; the pool is the whole account's, so one host (config/gh-daemon.json) watches it.
ALARM_CMD = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_GH_ALARM_CMD") or Path.home() / ".local/bin/tell-coordinator")
NOTICES_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("NOTICES_DIR") or Path.home() / ".local/state/notices")
ALARMS_FILE = "alarms.json"  # when each alarm was last sent, so a restart does not send it again
ALARM_EVERY = 3600.0
ALARM_REST_LEFT = 1000  # the pool alarm fires below this much REST left on the poorest core counter
ALARM_TREND_MIN = 300.0  # seconds of readings in one window a drain projection needs
ALARM_TREND_KEEP = 900.0  # seconds of readings the projection is drawn over
ALARM_FIRE_WITHIN = 600.0  # a pool projected empty within this, or under the REST reserve, is a FIRE
# The pace alarm: the pool draining faster than this share of its hourly limit (policy alarms.paceShare). Half of
# 5000 is 42 a minute. On 2026-11-10 (11:23Z) the pool drained at 158 a minute and the alarm said so only once it
# projected dry 25 minutes out; at half the limit there is still an hour's room to find the caller.
ALARM_PACE_SHARE = 0.5
# Who spent the pool on this host, for the alarm to name: leo-workspace tools/gh_quota.py `usage --json`, which counts
# every caller on the login here (the gh shim's sessions and tools, this daemon, the lane-gh broker's VMs). This
# daemon's own routes are a fraction of it (11:23Z: about 13 a minute of the 158).
SPENDERS_CMD = os.environ.get("GLAEDA_GH_SPENDERS_CMD") or os.fspath(Path.home() / ".local/bin/gh-quota")
SPENDERS_TIMEOUT = 10.0
SPENDERS_TOP = 3
# How stale this host's view of the shared quota may get before GET /rate_limit refreshes it. That
# endpoint reports every family at once and costs no primary quota, so the only cost of a short
# interval is the secondary limit, which one call every two minutes is nowhere near. 0 turns it off.
BUDGET_REFRESH = 120.0
BUDGET_RETRY = 900.0  # after a refused /rate_limit, how long before asking again
REST_COUNTERS = 4  # most core counters budget.json keeps (GitHub showed two per token on 2026-10-30)
RESET_JITTER = 2  # seconds: two readings whose resets differ by at most this are one core counter
# The REST paths GitHub always answered from one core counter, the "actions" one (X-RateLimit-Reset of real answers,
# cosmic-gray 2026-11-04 06:15-06:20Z, 28 of 28): a run, its attempts and artifacts, and workflows (a list, one, its
# runs, its dispatches). Pulls, a repo, check runs, the runs list, a run's jobs and /rate_limit answered mostly from
# the other counter and now and then from this one, so no reading of theirs can name a counter. A reading from one of
# these paths tags its counter "actions" in restCounters, so gh-quota (leo-workspace tools/gh_quota.py rest_reading)
